body:
The current readable encoders change finite JavaScript Number values into strings when their decimal integer spelling exceeds `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`. This affects existing numeric values (for example amounts or counters), independently of precision already lost before serialization.

Reproduced with `lino-objects-codec@0.9.5`, `links-notation@0.31.3`, Node 26.13.0:

```js
import { encode, decode, encodeLine, decodeLine } from 'lino-objects-codec';
const value = { count: 1e18, nested: [-9007199254740992] };
console.log(decode({ notation: encode({ obj: value }) }));
console.log(decodeLine({ notation: encodeLine({ obj: value }) }));
// Both: { count: '1000000000000000000', nested: ['-9007199254740992'] }
// Expected: the original Number values and types.
```

Root cause: `src/readable.js`'s `formatNumber` returns `String(value)`, while `refToValue` deliberately preserves unsafe bare integers as strings. Encoding `1e18` as `1e+18` round-trips as a Number. A suggested change in `formatNumber`, after its existing special-number handling:

```js
if (Number.isInteger(value) && !Number.isSafeInteger(value)) {
  return value.toExponential();
}
```

Please preserve numeric-looking string keys and values, bigint semantics, nested arrays/objects and safe integer formatting. Add readable and single-line regression cases for positive/negative unsafe integer Numbers, `1e18`, `1000000000000000100`, safe boundary values and matching strings. Compact/binary typed values already retain the Number type.

Workaround in [meta-sovereign PR 57](https://github.com/link-foundation/meta-sovereign/pull/57): use exponent notation only for unsafe integer Numbers through a shared adapter covering HTTP, sync, text-store records and recovery encoding. Ordinary documents retain upstream formatting. The bounded offline reproduction is `experiments/issue-56/lino-unsafe-integer-probe.mjs`; the regression is `js/tests/lino-transport.test.js` (`wire and HTTP replies preserve large numbers and numeric-looking keys`).

`encode()` produces the readable indented form settled in #37. `encode_compact()` produces a single line, but it is an alias of `encode_obfuscated()` (`src/lib.rs:489`) and base64-encodes every string. So the two available forms are *readable but multi-line* and *single-line but unreadable*, and there is no readable single-line encoder.

That combination is missing for a real and common shape: an append-only log, one record per line.

## Why one record per line is not a style preference

A log of this kind is appended a record at a time and compacted by scanning for a newline after a byte floor. Both operations depend on the record boundary being the line boundary:

- appending is a single write with no need to re-read or re-indent what is already there;
- compaction cuts at a newline and cannot split a record;
- `grep`, `tail -f`, `wc -l` and every log collector treat one line as one event.

The indented form from #37 spans a line per field, so a record no longer maps to a line and all of the above break. The base64 form keeps one line but destroys readability — which is the thing #37 was filed to obtain.

## What happens without it

A downstream project (link-assistant/router) needed exactly this and, finding no upstream API, wrote its own single-line emitter. It invented a field marker to disambiguate a pair from an array:

```
((:"bytes" 2827) (:"complete" true) (:"phase" "stream_end"))
```

Verified against `links-notation` 0.14.0 and `lino-objects-codec` 0.4.1:

```rust
links_notation::parse_lino(line)
// Err(Syntax error: TakeWhile1)

lino_objects_codec::decode(line)
// Ok(Array([
//   Array([String(":"), String("bytes"), Int(2827)]),
//   ...
// ]))
```

The notation parser rejects it, and this codec decodes each field into a three-element array instead of an object. The file round-trips only through that project's private inverse; to every other reader it is either invalid or the wrong shape.

That is not a mistake worth blaming downstream — it is what a missing API produces. The same gap will produce the same divergence in the next project that stores a log.

## What is being asked

A readable single-line encoder, and its exact inverse:

```rust
pub fn encode_line(value: &LinoValue) -> String;
pub fn decode_line(notation: &str) -> Result<LinoValue, CodecError>;
```

Properties:

- output contains no newline, so one record is one line;
- strings keep their own characters — only what would break the framing or the parse is escaped (`"`, `\`, newline, carriage return);
- numbers, booleans and null stay bare, so the type survives the round trip;
- output parses with `links_notation::parse_lino`;
- `decode(encode_line(v))` yields the same structure as `decode(encode(v))` — the single-line form is the same document, differently laid out, not a different dialect.

## The ambiguity that has to be answered

Written flat and without a marker, a pair and a one-element array collide:

```
(("") )     the pair whose key is ""  —  and  the array holding ""
```

The downstream project answered it with a `:` prefix, which is what put it outside the notation. Options worth weighing here, where the decision belongs:

1. **Structural disambiguation.** `((bytes 2827) (complete true))` already decodes to `Array([Object(…), Object(…)])` today — verified — so the ordinary case needs no marker at all. Only the empty-key case is genuinely ambiguous, and it may be rare enough to reject explicitly rather than design around.
2. **A marker that is part of the notation**, chosen here and documented, so tooling knows it.
3. **A distinct empty-object/empty-array spelling**, as #37 already needed for `(#a)` / `(#o)`.

## Suggested tests

- `encode_line` output contains no newline, for nested objects and arrays alike;
- `parse_lino(encode_line(v))` succeeds;
- `decode(encode_line(v)) == decode(encode(v))` for objects, arrays, nesting, and the empty collections;
- a string containing quotes, backslashes, newlines and non-ASCII text round-trips exactly and stays on one line;
- whichever answer is chosen for the empty key, it round-trips or is rejected with a clear error.

## Note

Found while auditing what a downstream deployment writes to disk: a 1.7 GB log documented as links notation which the notation's own parser cannot read.
